    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d856">

                <pb facs="0991" n="879" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg879"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.6363 Manchuria/136: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-08T17:00:00+09:00">February 8, 1935—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 8—9:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>27. 1. On February 6, Kurusu<anchor n="74" xml:id="d856-f1"/> stated to Sansom of
                    the British Embassy that the foreign oil companies were still objecting to the
                    monopoly as such and were not attempting to make business arrangements within
                    the monopoly. He believed this to be arguing in circles as the monopoly plan
                    would doubtless be carried out. He advised the foreign oil companies to discuss
                    the matter purely on a commercial basis leaving legal aspects to be discussed by
                    the Government.</p>
                <pb facs="0992" n="880" xml:id="pg880"/>
                <p>2. Sansom replied that, although he did not know if the foreign oil companies had
                    reached a final decision, he had gathered from their representatives when he
                    last saw them that if the Manchurian authorities carried out the monopoly scheme
                    the foreign oil companies could see no alternative as a purely commercial
                    proposition except to dispose of their investments in Manchuria.</p>
                <p>3. The above reply of Sansom was prepared beforehand at a meeting of the special
                    representatives of the foreign oil interests.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d856-f1" n="74" xml:id="d856-f1-content">
                <p>Saburo Kurusu, director of the Commercial Affairs Bureau, Japanese Foreign
                    Office.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
